<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kumtor Gold Mine</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kumtor Gold Mine is an open-pit gold mine high in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a> mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/issyk-kul">Lake Issyk-Kul</a>, at about 4,000 metres. Developed with a Canadian company and producing from 1997, it became the country's largest industrial enterprise and a large share of its exports and output. The Kyrgyz government took control of the mine in 2021 and full ownership in 2022.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kumtor-gold-mine.jpg" alt="Kumtor Gold Mine" width="320" height="141" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael Karavanov · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kumtor_Gold_Mine_-_panoramio_-_Michael_Karavanov.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kumtor is one of the largest gold mines in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, and at 13,000 feet one of the highest anywhere. The deposit is worked as an open pit by drilling and blasting, with trucks and loaders or shovels carrying the ore to a crusher and then to a mill, where the gold is extracted by the carbon-in-leach process. The mill is rated to treat about 16,000 tonnes of ore a day. By 30 June 2022 the mine had produced over 13.8 million ounces of gold. In 2020 its operations accounted for 12.5% of the gross domestic product of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a> and 23.3% of its industrial output.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A geophysical expedition of the State Geological Committee of the Kirghiz Soviet Socialist Republic discovered the deposit in 1978. In 1992 the new Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a> and the Cameco Corporation signed an agreement forming the Kumtor Gold Project, and Cameco became its full owner that year. Construction began in 1993, and commercial production started in May 1997. The government's dealings with Centerra Gold over the mine were long in dispute: in 2013 Parliament rejected a draft memorandum for a 50/50 joint venture, demanding a Kyrgyz share of 67 percent, and in December 2015 the government withdrew from the talks.</p><p>The disputes went to international courts until the Strategic Environment and Investment Agreement between Centerra Gold and the government settled the arbitration and environmental claims. On 16 November 2016 amendments to the Water Code permitted commercial activity near the Davydov and Lysyi glaciers. From 2012 campaigners called for nationalisation, accusing Centerra Gold of environmental violations and corruption. On 14 May 2021 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadyr-japarov">Sadyr Japarov</a> signed a law allowing temporary state control, and on 17 May the Cabinet of Ministers placed the Kumtor Gold Company under external management, appointing Tengiz Bolturuk to run it. Centerra Gold answered with arbitration, and one source described the action as an expropriation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kumtor Gold Mine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The economy relies on the <b>Kumtor</b> gold mine, agriculture and remittances from migrants in Russia.</blockquote><a href="/state/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a></li></ul></section>
